{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 1. Executive Summary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 OpenClaw is an AI agent platform running on a Linux gateway server accessible via Tailscale. It manages Claude-based agents, executes tools, handles memory, schedules cron jobs, and communicates via multiple channels (Telegram, webchat). The current interface is a web-based Control UI \u8212- functional but fundamentally limited for desktop use.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This document specifies a native macOS application to replace the web UI as the primary desktop interface for OpenClaw. The app delivers a first-class macOS experience: menu bar access, native notifications, real-time agent transparency, keyboard-driven workflow, and a direct WebSocket connection to the gateway \u8212- no third-party relay required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Target users:} OpenClaw operators \u8212- individuals running personal AI agent setups who interact with their agents throughout the workday from a Mac.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Primary goals:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Replace the web UI with a native macOS application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Provide real-time visibility into agent activity (tool calls, subagent spawns, file operations)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Enable asynchronous workflows with macOS-native notification/approval flows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Deliver an experience that feels like Slack or Linear \u8212- not a wrapped browser tab\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Phased delivery:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Phase 1 (MVP):} Functional chat client with menu bar access, session management, streaming WebSocket chat, markdown rendering, file attachments, and notifications. Buildable in 6\u8211-8 weeks by one Swift engineer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Phase 2:} Three-pane layout with live Work Panel, Timeline Replay, Agent Roster, Artifact Library, Steer Controls, multi-gateway support, Apple Watch companion, and full keyboard workflow. 3\u8211-4 months additional work.\sa180\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 2. Problem Statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 2.1 Current State\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 OpenClaw\u8217's primary user interface is a web-based Control UI served at port 18789 on the gateway. Operators interact with agents by navigating to {\f1 https://ubuntu-ct.tailb4a099.ts.net:18789} in a browser.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This works. It is not good enough.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 2.2 Specific Deficiencies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b No native macOS integration:}\line The web UI cannot place itself in the menu bar, register global keyboard shortcuts, access the macOS notification center, integrate with Spotlight, support drag-and-drop file operations, or participate in Handoff. It is a website. macOS does not know it exists.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b No real-time task transparency:}\line When an agent executes a multi-step task \u8212- reading files, spawning subagents, calling tools, writing output \u8212- the operator sees nothing until the final response arrives. For tasks that take 30\u8211-120 seconds, this is a black box. There is no way to know if the agent is working, stuck, or about to do something undesirable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Telegram timeout issues:}\line Telegram is currently used as an async channel, but it imposes response timeouts that break long-running tasks. Operators get disconnected from in-progress work. The conversation history fragments. There is no approval flow for sensitive actions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Workflow friction:}\line Switching to a browser tab, navigating to the control UI, and typing in a web form is the wrong abstraction for an always-on AI assistant. The tool should be as accessible as a Spotlight search \u8212- a keyboard shortcut away, then gone when you\u8217're done.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 2.3 What \u8220"Fixed\u8221" Looks Like\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A native macOS app that:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Lives in the menu bar and is accessible in under one second via keyboard shortcut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Streams agent responses in real-time with visible tool execution steps\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Surfaces approval requests as macOS actionable notifications\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Handles long-running tasks natively with a live work panel \u8212- no timeouts, no black box\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Stores chat history locally so it persists regardless of gateway state\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Feels like an app, not a workaround\sa180\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 3. MVP Specification \u8212- Phase 1\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Phase 1 delivers a fully functional, shippable chat client. It covers the core loop: connect, chat, see responses, get notified. Everything in Phase 1 should be buildable and testable in isolation.\par}
