{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 3.2 Menu Bar App\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The app runs as a menu bar extra ({\f1 LSUIElement = YES} in Info.plist). It does not appear in the Dock. It does not appear in the app switcher (Cmd+Tab). It is always running in the background.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Menu bar icon:} A custom icon representing OpenClaw (the claw mark). It has two states: - {\b Active/connected:} Full opacity - {\b Disconnected/error:} Dimmed or with a warning badge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Click behavior:} Single-click opens the main window as a popover anchored to the menu bar icon. The popover is detachable \u8212- if the user drags it, it becomes a floating window that persists independently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Detached window behavior:} The window is a standard {\f1 NSWindow} with {\f1 NSWindowStyleMaskTitled | NSWindowStyleMaskClosable | NSWindowStyleMaskResizable}. Closing the window returns to popover mode (does not quit the app).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b App quit:} Via the Gear menu \u8594 ? Quit, or via right-click on the menu bar icon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 3.3 Gateway Connection Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 3.3.1 First Launch Experience\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On first launch, the app displays a setup sheet:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 +------------------------------------------------+\line
|  Connect to OpenClaw Gateway                   |\line
|                                                |\line
|  Gateway URL                                   |\line
|  [ https://ubuntu-ct.tailb4a099.ts.net _____ ] |\line
|                                                |\line
|  Auth Token                                    |\line
|  [ \u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ?\u8226 ? ] |\line
|                                                |\line
|  [Test Connection]          [Save & Connect]   |\line
+------------------------------------------------+\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b URL field:} Standard text field. Accepts {\f1 https://} or {\f1 wss://} prefix. The app normalizes internally \u8212- {\f1 https://host} becomes {\f1 wss://host} for WebSocket connections and {\f1 https://host} for REST calls.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Token field:} Secure text field (no autocomplete, no paste suggestion). Password-style masking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Test Connection button:} Makes a lightweight REST probe to {\f1 GET /api/status} (or equivalent health endpoint) with the provided credentials. Shows inline success/failure without saving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Save & Connect:} Validates URL format, saves to Keychain (see Section 8.2), initiates WebSocket connection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 3.3.2 Connection Management (Settings)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Settings panel (Cmd+,) exposes: - Current gateway URL (editable) - Token (show/hide toggle, re-enter to change) - Connection status - Disconnect button - Add Gateway button (placeholder for Phase 2 multi-gateway)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 3.4 WebSocket Chat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 3.4.1 Connection Lifecycle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab App launches \u8594 ? read gateway URL and token from Keychain\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab If both present: initiate WebSocket connection immediately\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab Connection state: {\f1 disconnected \u8594 ? connecting \u8594 ? connected}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 4.\tx360\tab On connection drop: enter {\f1 reconnecting} state with exponential backoff (1s, 2s, 4s, 8s, 16s, cap at 60s)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 5.\tx360\tab On successful reconnect: reload session list, resume active session\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 3.4.2 Message Flow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Sending a message:} 1. User types in text field, presses Return or clicks Send 2. Message appended to local SQLite store with status {\f1 sending} 3. WebSocket message sent to gateway (see protocol in Section 5.3) 4. Gateway ACKs message \u8594 ? status updated to {\f1 sent} 5. Agent begins responding \u8594 ? streaming tokens arrive 6. Each token appended to in-progress response bubble in real-time 7. Stream complete signal \u8594 ? response finalized in SQLite with status {\f1 complete}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Receiving a message:} 1. WebSocket event arrives 2. Parse JSON envelope (see Section 5.3) 3. Route to appropriate session by {\f1 session_id} 4. If session not active in UI: queue notification 5. Render in chat view\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 3.4.3 Streaming Text Rendering\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Streaming tokens must render with no perceptible lag. Implementation:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Use {\f1 @Published var streamBuffer: String} on the message view model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Each incoming token triggers {\f1 streamBuffer += token}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab SwiftUI {\f1 Text(streamBuffer)} re-renders automatically\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab For markdown: buffer raw text during streaming, parse markdown only on stream completion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Intermediate state: render as plain text with a blinking cursor indicator\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab On stream complete: replace plain text with fully parsed markdown view\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This avoids expensive markdown re-parses on every token while keeping the streaming feel.\par}
